# Using Session Data on WebSocket Handshake

When a logged-in user navigates to a real-time section of your application, the transition from standard HTTP requests to WebSockets often hits a wall: the handshake request headers lack cookie data. This limitation exists because the `Sec-WebSocket-Key`, `Upgrade`, and other specific headers define the protocol negotiation, not the session context. Even if you attempt to force JavaScript to send cookies, `httpOnly` flags prevent client-side access to those values for transmission via custom headers or form fields during the upgrade process. The browser simply won't include the session cookie in the WebSocket handshake unless the server explicitly instructs it to do so.

The solution relies on understanding how the HTTP handshake precedes the WebSocket upgrade. Before a `Sec-WebSocket-Key` is processed, the request is treated as a standard HTTP GET or POST. If your server framework supports itâsuch as Laravelâyou can configure the server to include specific session cookies in the outgoing response headers that influence the client's behavior, or more commonly, you simply need to ensure the server accepts cookies from the initial handshake if they are present.

However, in many scenarios, especially when using older frameworks like the one referenced in the source material (`PHPWebSocket`), the raw WebSocket library might strip cookies or fail to read them automatically due to how the underlying PHP socket handles the upgrade. The most robust approach is not to rely on the client sending a cookie, but rather to leverage the HTTP session that already exists before the upgrade occurs.

If you are using a framework like Laravel, you can configure the WebSocket service to read from the `$_SESSION` or the database directly upon connection. But if you must identify the user strictly via the handshake without relying on a pre-existing server-side session mapping to an IP address, you have to look at the HTTP headers available during the upgrade request.

The standard header `Cookie` is often missing in the raw output shown in the question because the client hasn't sent it yet, or the library ignores it. To fix this, you must ensure that your server framework does not strip the `Cookie` header before processing the WebSocket handshake. In PHP, when using the native stream socket wrapper or a library like Ratchet (which is the modern replacement for older libraries like `PHPWebSocket`), the underlying HTTP request object retains the `Cookie` header.

Here is how you can access that data in a PHP-based WebSocket server:

```php
// Inside your WebSocket onOpen handler or the underlying HTTP handshake listener
$request = $this->httpServer->getHttpServer()->getQueryString(); // Or getRawRequest() depending on library
$headers = $request->headers;

$cookieValue = $headers->get('Cookie');

if ($cookieValue) {
    // Parse the cookie string to find your session ID
    // Be careful, as this is a raw string.
    $parts = preg_split('/\s*[;,]\s*/', $cookieValue);
    foreach ($parts as $part) {
        if (strpos($part, 'PHPSESSID=') !== false || strpos($part, 'session_id=') !== false) {
            list($_, $sessionId) = explode('=', $part, 2);
            // Now you have the session ID to map to your user database
        }
    }
}
```

If the `Cookie` header is genuinely absent because the client hasn't sent it (common with strict CORS or specific library implementations), you cannot force the browser to send an `httpOnly` cookie in a way that bypasses standard HTTP protocols. The only way to make this work is if your server framework automatically attaches the session ID to the connection metadata before the WebSocket object is instantiated.

In Laravel, for instance, you can configure your WebSocket service to accept connections and immediately resolve the authenticated user or session from the request attributes:

```php
// Example conceptual logic within a Laravel WebSocket listener
public function onOpen($connection) {
    // Ensure the connection has access to the HTTP request context
    if ($this->app->request->hasSession()) {
        $session = $this->app->request->session();
        $userId = $session->getId(); // Or retrieve authenticated user ID

        $this->broadcastChannel->setUserId($userId);
    }
}
```

The critical trade-off here is performance and complexity. Parsing cookies manually adds overhead, and relying on the `Cookie` header assumes your WebSocket library preserves the raw HTTP headers during the upgrade phase. Older libraries like the one linked in the source material might not support this out of the box, requiring you to switch to a more modern stack like Ratchet or Laravel's built-in WebSocket support, which handles session persistence much more gracefully by binding the WebSocket connection directly to the Laravel application instance.

Ultimately, if the handshake request lacks the `Cookie` header entirely due to library limitations, the architecture needs to shift from "grabbing the ID at the handshake" to "associating the connection with the user immediately upon upgrade." This usually involves checking the `Origin` header for subdomain hints or maintaining a server-side mapping of IP addresses to sessions, though the latter is less secure. The most secure and standard path is ensuring your WebSocket library reads the `Cookie` header from the initial HTTP request and passes it to your application logic before the socket is fully established.
